raph database is really another whole  side of no sequel databases that is  pretty much unrelated to any of the no  sequel databases I've discussed so far  and what a graph database really is it  is exactly what it sounds like it's a  way of querying vertices and nodes along  a graph in a really efficient way so if  you think about problems such as  traversing a social network this is the  problem that like any problem can be  solved relationally but it becomes  unwieldy after a certain point in time  and so what a graph database is as a way  of very conveniently asking those kinds  of problems and getting answers for  those kinds of problems in a convenient  way for your application well and our  DBMS is essentially a relationship among  tables so if you think about how what a  graph is a graph tends to be a an  arbitrarily connected set of points so  if you wanted to represent an arbitrary  an arbitrary set of points and how they  connect in a database you typically  choose one table and then describe the  relationship between rows in that table  which means joining again and again and  again further out you want to explore in  that relationship it's something that  can be done but becomes extremely huge  in terms of the problem especially if  your graph is heavily connected so we  tend to use graph databases for two  kinds of problems one for trying to  determine you're trying to move along  your social network trying to find  friends of friends and friends and what  they might be interested in and also we  use it for various types of proximity  algorithms trying to find a best  possible match for you as a person or  the best possible product or  relationships between products for  product advisory services these kind of  databases are extremely useful for  constructing algorithms like that one  last area where they can come up a lot  is in a data visualization there's a lot  of different types of data where it's  not entirely obvious how things connect  together and a graph database allows you  to sort of probe the connections between  things and follow them in real time a  graph database is really a different  kind of beast than the other no sequel  databases graph database is obviously  very good at quoting a graph and  interpreting a graph any of these  databases can do it but if you think  about what the document databases are  doing and the key value stores are doing  they're functioning in large measure to  distribute things across a cluster of  servers a graph because it's by its  nature arbitrarily connected means that  it's fairly difficult to solve that  problem in the abstract across a network  of machines so graph databases with some  exceptions but tend to focus on on  problem sets that can be constrained in  the size of one machine so on some  levels you know some no sequel databases  besides graph databases particularly  document databases also work perfectly  great in a single on a single node a  single replicated node that a single  node because it's so convenient to  develop against them but it's a question  of what are you trying to develop if  you're trying to develop something like  an application that saves different  kinds of data and then searches by it a  document database is often an extremely  convenient way to perform that whereas  be trying to probe relationships between  things especially relationships that go  beyond sort of an immediate set of  neighbors a graph is a better choice
 

